  "heading": "\\equivalence.relation:on{X}",
  "heading_latex": "\\textrm{equivalence relation on }X",
  "body_text": null,
  "page": null,
  "source": "[\\equivalence.relation:on{X}]\nDeclares: R is \\relation:on{X}\nwhen: X is \\set\nsatisfies:\n. [reflexive]\n  forAll: x \"in\" X\n  then: R(x, x)\n. [symmetric]\n  forAll: x, y \"in\" R\n  where: R(x, y)\n  then: R(y, x)\n. [transitive]\n  forAll: x, y, z \"in\" R\n  where:\n  . R(x, y)\n  . R(y, z)\n  then: R(x, z)\nDocumented:\n. called: \"equivalence relation on $X?$\"\n. description: \"An equivalence relation on $X$ is a relation that is reflexive\n                ($x \\mathrel{R} x$), symmetric (if $x \\mathrel{R} y$ then $y\n                \\mathrel{R} x$), and transitive (if $x \\mathrel{R} y$ and $y\n                \\mathrel{R} z$ then $x \\mathrel{R} z$). Such a relation sorts the\n                elements of $X$ into classes of mutually related elements.\"\nId: \"2394a97b-5960-4919-98db-b95ec3870343\"",
